  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "By adopting a feminist perspective and engaging with relational approaches to space, this article examines how care is enacted through urban policies and spatial practices in the context of the contemporary crisis of social reproduction. Based on a qualitative analysis of institutional documents and project materials, the paper examines four ongoing initiatives in Montevideo - the Plan Nacional de Cuidados, Cuidados en el B, Fincas Recuperadas and Reactor - to explore how care is embedded in urban arrangements that reorganise social reproduction beyond neoliberal logics, outlining new urban approaches that privilege communal practices and everyday life. We argue that the interaction between national policy frameworks and neighbourhood-scale interventions enables these experiences to territorialise care by extending it beyond assistance and reframing access to urban space, housing, and everyday services through principles of co-responsibility and collective use. Situating these processes within broader debates on social reproduction and urban studies, the article contributes to contemporary debates on care by grounding it in grassroot practices and interpreting the city as a shared platform where social relations are constitutive.",
